# 12. Risk-adjusted profitability

The firm should calculate corridor contribution in layers to avoid double counting.

## Layer 1: Operating contribution

\[
\text{Operating contribution}
=
\text{Fees}
+
\text{FX spread}
-
\text{Partner fees}
-
\text{Bank and rail fees}
-
\text{Variable operations}
-
\text{Variable compliance}
-
\text{Technology usage}
\]

## Layer 2: Expected-loss contribution

Subtract:

- Fraud losses;
- Chargebacks and returns;
- Partner default expected loss;
- Customer compensation;
- Expected FX slippage;
- Expected impairment or devaluation loss.

## Layer 3: Capital and liquidity contribution

Subtract:

- Funding cost;
- Liquidity opportunity cost;
- Economic capital charge;
- Collateral and reserve carry cost;
- Restricted-cash cost;
- Hedging carry and margin cost.

Safeguarding balances should not be treated as a cost twice: the model should distinguish the balance legally required to protect customers from the incremental liquidity or capital cost caused by holding it.

## Minimum decision rules

Illustrative group standards:

- Scale corridor: risk-adjusted contribution margin at least **0.50% of transaction value** and at least **15% return on allocated capital**;
- Defend corridor: positive contribution and at least **8% return on allocated capital**;
- Fix corridor: negative or below-hurdle economics only with approved remediation;
- Exit trigger: negative contribution for two consecutive quarters or failure to meet the remediation plan;
- No expansion if stress-case contribution is negative and liquidity shortfall exceeds available contingency funding.

Thresholds should be calibrated to the firm’s cost of capital, but must be set before performance is measured.

